Andrew Kerr wrote:

> I've been put off implementing my ideas for patches and interfaces by
> Nethack's not particularly pleasant code base, and version dependant
> bones "format". I've come to the conclusion that NH needs to be
> rewritten in a high-level OOP language and that Python may be ideal.
> With a good OO design, once the core "engine" is written the community
> ahould be able to add items, spells etc easily: this would be one of the
> design goals in fact, for easy modding. I even had this silly idea that
> I could do it. Of course, the DevTeam's FAQ makes it clear that they
> weren't going to do a rewrite... but they were talking about Java, not
> Python.
> 
> There are two ways to combine Python and C:
> 
> 1. C engine with embedded Python script for game logic. The way most
> commercial games are written. Note that Lua is designed for this task
> and may be a better choice. I believe ADOM does this, using Lua.
> 
> 2. Almost entire program in Python, with calls to native C-compiled
> functions for demanding code, e.g. line-of-sight and shortest-path. Note
> that Python has no performance penalty for this.
> 
> I'd go for option 2. Option 1 may allow for reusing more of the existing
> code base.
> 
> Why Python?
> 
> # Highly portable
> 
> # Easy to use (a subjective judgement)
> 
> # Extensive OOP design
> 
> # Extensive libraries, e.g. for IP sockets
> 
> # No performance penalty for calling native code
> 
> # Bindings to numerous cross platform native code GUI toolkits (Tk, GTK,
> Qt, wxWidgets)
> 
> # Bytecode interpreted for easy development and easy modding
> 
> Well, the possibility of Nethack being rewritten in a high-level OOP
> language has me giddy with excitement ... I think I need a lie down :)

I've had the same thought about re-writing nethack in an OOP language. I've
generally considered C++, because that and Java are the OOP languages I'm
most familiar with, and C++ is more closely related to the language of the
existing codebase. I personally think that re-designing nethack in an OOP
language would be an interesting mental exercise, as well as adding quite a
bit of customizability to the game, if done correctly. I'd probably need to
study the way the existing code works before seriously starting a new
design, but perhaps not.

Anyone else out there interested in the exercise?


